PUBLIC HISTORY

The public Git history is a curated reconstruction from private source
archives. Each reconstructed commit represents a real source state or a
coherent capability milestone. Commit messages record the source repository,
source revision, and source author date. Public commit hashes and committer
times belong to the reconstruction.

The graph omits credentials, production records, generated exports, obsolete
private policy material, historical business assets, and partial or immediately
superseded fixups. Adjacent source commits may appear as one milestone when they
form one coherent change. The original uneven cadence remains visible. Dates
were not redistributed to manufacture a uniform contribution history.

The 2024 static prototype remains a separate historical project and is not a
code ancestor. The first public commit is a sanitized snapshot of the final
2025 two-application predecessor. Selected 2026 workspace states then show the
development of the current product.

Repository-authored source represented by this graph is offered under
AGPL-3.0-only as of publication. A historical author date does not mean that a
snapshot was public or AGPL-licensed on that date.

